1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,855
Osaka. The eighteenth of September, 1981.

2
00:00:04,147 --> 00:00:06,571
An old man opens the morning paper.

3
00:00:06,571 --> 00:00:10,172
Somewhere on an inside page
there is a short report.

4
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:15,455
Gold has been found in the far south
of Japan, under a hill in Kagoshima.

5
00:00:15,747 --> 00:00:19,797
Millions of people read that
paper. Most of them turn the page.

6
00:00:20,113 --> 00:00:23,835
He is eighty-four years old. He
has been rich more than once.

7
00:00:23,835 --> 00:00:28,595
He has lost everything more than once.
And he reads the article again, slowly,

8
00:00:28,595 --> 00:00:31,463
the way he has read
everything for fifty years.

9
00:00:31,780 --> 00:00:35,555
Within days, he is on his way
to see the hill for himself.

10
00:00:35,847 --> 00:00:36,707
Within months,

11
00:00:36,707 --> 00:00:41,745
he will make one of the biggest single
profits any individual has ever made on the

12
00:00:41,745 --> 00:00:43,097
Japanese stock market.

13
00:00:43,413 --> 00:00:46,338
And within ten years, it will all be gone.

14
00:00:51,647 --> 00:00:54,072
His name was Ginzō Korekawa.

15
00:00:54,380 --> 00:00:57,405
In Japan they called
him the last speculator.

16
00:00:57,713 --> 00:01:01,713
His schooling ended with elementary
school. He did not drink.

17
00:01:02,013 --> 00:01:04,738
He carried the same worn bag for years.

18
00:01:05,047 --> 00:01:06,871
And in 1983, at eighty-five,

19
00:01:06,871 --> 00:01:11,039
he topped the national list of
Japan's highest-income taxpayers.

20
00:01:11,039 --> 00:01:15,272
Above the heads of the great
companies' founders. Above everyone.

21
00:01:15,580 --> 00:01:20,630
But when he died, he left almost nothing.
A small flat that he shared with his wife.

22
00:01:20,947 --> 00:01:25,747
So how does a man make a fortune at
eighty-four, and lose it again before he dies?

23
00:01:26,047 --> 00:01:29,772
To understand that trade, you
have to go back almost a century.

24
00:01:29,772 --> 00:01:32,847
Because he had been preparing
for it his whole life.

25
00:01:34,147 --> 00:01:38,873
He was born in 1897, in Akō, a
fishing town on Japan's Inland Sea.

26
00:01:38,873 --> 00:01:43,097
He was the youngest of seven
brothers. The family was poor.

27
00:01:43,413 --> 00:01:47,827
At fourteen he went to work as an
apprentice at a trading firm in Kobe.

28
00:01:47,827 --> 00:01:50,438
A few years later, the firm went bankrupt.

29
00:01:50,747 --> 00:01:55,408
He watched the men who worked there lose
everything because of decisions they had

30
00:01:55,408 --> 00:01:59,897
never made. He decided he would never
again depend on someone else's business.

31
00:02:00,213 --> 00:02:04,463
At seventeen, according to his
biographer, he set out for London.

32
00:02:04,780 --> 00:02:06,981
He got as far as Dalian, in China.

33
00:02:06,981 --> 00:02:11,255
Then the First World War broke out,
and the road to Europe closed.

34
00:02:11,547 --> 00:02:14,076
So he followed the Japanese army instead.

35
00:02:14,076 --> 00:02:19,072
He walked behind it to Qingdao, running
so short of money that he nearly starved.

36
00:02:19,380 --> 00:02:23,480
Then he talked his way into supplying
it, with food and with timber.

37
00:02:23,780 --> 00:02:28,900
His biographer tells one story that
explains the rest of his life. In Qingdao,

38
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:33,955
the small old coins people used every
day were made of copper, zinc and lead.

39
00:02:34,247 --> 00:02:38,815
He worked out that the metal in them was
worth more than double the money stamped

40
00:02:38,815 --> 00:02:41,747
on them. So he bought coins,
and sold them as metal.

41
00:02:42,047 --> 00:02:46,447
Everyone else saw small change. He
saw what it was actually made of.

42
00:02:46,747 --> 00:02:48,518
By nineteen, he was rich.

43
00:02:48,518 --> 00:02:53,972
Then, in December 1916, a sudden market
crash took his business down with it.

44
00:02:54,280 --> 00:02:57,855
He went home with nothing. It
would not be the last time.

45
00:02:59,147 --> 00:03:02,572
In Osaka he started again. He traded iron.

46
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:05,905
He ran a factory that
coated steel wire in zinc,

47
00:03:05,905 --> 00:03:10,505
and by his biographer's account it
employed two hundred and sixty people.

48
00:03:10,813 --> 00:03:13,515
In 1923, an earthquake flattened Tokyo.

49
00:03:13,515 --> 00:03:18,641
A city of millions needed roofs and
nails overnight, and he supplied them.

50
00:03:18,641 --> 00:03:19,888
He made a fortune.

51
00:03:20,180 --> 00:03:24,783
According to his biographer, he then
gave half of it to the Osaka government,

52
00:03:24,783 --> 00:03:28,130
saying it was money made from
other people's misfortune.

53
00:03:28,447 --> 00:03:31,773
Then, in 1927, panic swept
through Japan's banks.

54
00:03:31,773 --> 00:03:35,710
Customers queued around the
block to take their money out,

55
00:03:35,710 --> 00:03:38,222
and bank after bank closed its doors.

56
00:03:38,513 --> 00:03:42,013
One of them was his. His
company went down with it.

57
00:03:42,313 --> 00:03:46,088
He was thirty. For the second
time, he had lost everything.

58
00:03:46,380 --> 00:03:51,505
Most men would have looked for a job. He
went to the library instead. And he stayed.

59
00:03:51,813 --> 00:03:53,682
For about three years, he read.

60
00:03:53,682 --> 00:03:58,263
Economics, history, the world's markets,
the papers of the great economists.

61
00:03:58,580 --> 00:04:01,657
He came out with one idea
that he never let go of.

62
00:04:01,657 --> 00:04:05,780
Depressions are not the end of the
world. They are part of a cycle.

63
00:04:05,780 --> 00:04:10,334
Booms follow busts, and busts follow
booms. Capitalism would not collapse.

64
00:04:10,334 --> 00:04:11,380
It would breathe.

65
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:16,180
If you understood the cycle, you did not
have to guess. You could wait for it.

66
00:04:17,480 --> 00:04:22,355
In 1931, at the bottom of the worst
depression the world had seen,

67
00:04:22,647 --> 00:04:26,372
he walked into the Osaka stock
exchange with seventy yen.

68
00:04:26,680 --> 00:04:29,619
By the end of the year,
he had seven thousand.

69
00:04:29,619 --> 00:04:33,580
A hundredfold, in a year when
almost everyone else was losing.

70
00:04:33,880 --> 00:04:38,759
Two years later, he opened his own economic
research institute. Not a brokerage.

71
00:04:38,759 --> 00:04:40,039
A research institute.

72
00:04:40,039 --> 00:04:44,430
He wanted to know where the economy
was going before he bought anything.

73
00:04:44,747 --> 00:04:48,540
In the 1930s, his biographer
says, he forecast that Britain,

74
00:04:48,540 --> 00:04:53,219
America and the Soviet Union would
rearm in Asia, and he said so publicly.

75
00:04:53,219 --> 00:04:56,253
The military police took
him in for questioning.

76
00:04:56,253 --> 00:04:58,972
A government official
had to vouch for him.

77
00:04:59,280 --> 00:05:02,545
Later writers summed up
his method in three rules.

78
00:05:02,545 --> 00:05:07,313
Choose your own stocks, never on a
tip. Forecast the economy years ahead.

79
00:05:07,313 --> 00:05:08,880
And never chase a price.

80
00:05:09,180 --> 00:05:14,480
In Japan they are known as the turtle's
three rules. Slow. Patient. Hard to kill.

81
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:19,420
He did not always follow his own
rules. In 1935, his biographer says,

82
00:05:19,420 --> 00:05:24,800
he was locked in a battle in the cotton
futures market against a powerful cotton

83
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:25,600
merchant.

84
00:05:25,713 --> 00:05:29,945
In a fight like that, the two sides
can agree to settle and walk away,

85
00:05:29,945 --> 00:05:31,638
the winner taking his gains.

86
00:05:31,947 --> 00:05:36,826
He was three million yen ahead. His
opponent offered to settle. He refused.

87
00:05:36,826 --> 00:05:38,647
He wanted to win completely.

88
00:05:38,947 --> 00:05:43,447
The market turned. The gains vanished,
and he ended the battle with a loss.

89
00:05:43,747 --> 00:05:48,872
He never forgot it. Later writers quote
his rule: selling is harder than buying.

90
00:05:50,180 --> 00:05:55,255
In 1938, he went into mining in
Korea, then a Japanese colony.

91
00:05:55,547 --> 00:05:58,922
The mines fed ironworks.
The war fed demand.

92
00:05:59,213 --> 00:06:01,623
By the war years, his biographer says,

93
00:06:01,623 --> 00:06:04,795
Korekawa Steel employed
about ten thousand people.

94
00:06:04,795 --> 00:06:07,713
It was one of the biggest
businesses in Korea.

95
00:06:08,013 --> 00:06:10,927
Then, in August 1945, Japan surrendered.

96
00:06:10,927 --> 00:06:14,497
Everything he had built
in Korea was confiscated.

97
00:06:14,497 --> 00:06:17,338
He was arrested and thrown into prison.

98
00:06:17,647 --> 00:06:19,922
He expected to be executed.

99
00:06:20,213 --> 00:06:24,296
Instead, his Korean colleagues and
workers petitioned for his release.

100
00:06:24,296 --> 00:06:28,263
He had never treated them as lesser
people, they said. He was freed.

101
00:06:28,580 --> 00:06:32,501
He sailed back to a defeated,
hungry Japan. He was forty-eight.

102
00:06:32,501 --> 00:06:34,680
For the third time, he had nothing.

103
00:06:35,980 --> 00:06:40,353
He did not go back to the market
straight away. The country was starving,

104
00:06:40,353 --> 00:06:44,905
so he studied farming, ways to grow two
crops of rice a year instead of one.

105
00:06:45,213 --> 00:06:50,142
Then he bought land on the edge of Osaka,
where he was sure the city would spread.

106
00:06:50,142 --> 00:06:50,942
It did.

107
00:06:50,847 --> 00:06:53,706
When he sold, he had
three hundred million yen.

108
00:06:53,706 --> 00:06:56,322
And at sixty-eight, he
went back to stocks.

109
00:06:56,613 --> 00:07:02,938
In 1976, aged seventy-nine, he put three
hundred million yen into Nihon Cement.

110
00:07:03,247 --> 00:07:06,480
Japan had just come through
the oil shock and a long slump.

111
00:07:06,480 --> 00:07:07,850
He had studied the cycle,

112
00:07:07,850 --> 00:07:11,247
and he was sure the country was
about to start building again.

113
00:07:11,547 --> 00:07:16,822
He was right. The gain was about three
billion yen. Ten times what he had put in.

114
00:07:17,113 --> 00:07:18,090
Two years later,

115
00:07:18,090 --> 00:07:22,975
he put more than a billion yen of his own
money into a foundation to pay for the

116
00:07:22,975 --> 00:07:26,638
education of children from poor
families. He was eighty-two.

117
00:07:26,947 --> 00:07:28,722
He was not finished.

118
00:07:30,013 --> 00:07:35,638
Which brings us back to that morning in
1981, and the small article about gold.

119
00:07:35,947 --> 00:07:39,672
The gold was at Hishikari,
in the hills of Kagoshima.

120
00:07:39,980 --> 00:07:41,091
Earlier that year,

121
00:07:41,091 --> 00:07:46,155
a government survey had drilled into a vein
of quartz that was astonishingly rich.

122
00:07:46,447 --> 00:07:50,747
A typical gold mine might get a few
grams of gold from a tonne of rock.

123
00:07:50,747 --> 00:07:53,897
Hishikari's ore would turn
out to hold around forty.

124
00:07:54,213 --> 00:07:58,838
And the mining rights belonged to
one company. Sumitomo Metal Mining.

125
00:07:59,147 --> 00:08:00,772
Here is why that mattered.

126
00:08:00,772 --> 00:08:04,772
A big discovery can change what
a whole mining company is worth.

127
00:08:04,772 --> 00:08:06,897
But the market had barely reacted.

128
00:08:07,213 --> 00:08:10,725
Sumitomo's shares were trading
at around two hundred yen,

129
00:08:10,725 --> 00:08:12,388
as if nothing had happened.

130
00:08:12,680 --> 00:08:15,380
Korekawa went to see the site himself.

131
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:17,450
Only one question mattered.

132
00:08:17,450 --> 00:08:22,105
Was this a lucky pocket of gold, or
a seam that ran on under the hills?

133
00:08:22,413 --> 00:08:26,863
The local mine office told him
further digging was unlikely to pay.

134
00:08:27,180 --> 00:08:31,350
He ignored them. He was sure the vein
ran on under the land next door.

135
00:08:31,350 --> 00:08:34,805
So he bought the mining rights
to the land next door, too.

136
00:08:35,113 --> 00:08:38,113
And he bought Sumitomo shares. Then more.

137
00:08:38,413 --> 00:08:40,488
Then more with borrowed money.

138
00:08:40,780 --> 00:08:44,815
At eighty-four, he was betting on
margin. If the price fell far enough,

139
00:08:44,815 --> 00:08:49,305
his broker would sell him out, and he
would lose far more than he started with.

140
00:08:49,613 --> 00:08:53,613
By the spring, he was one of the
company's largest shareholders.

141
00:08:53,913 --> 00:08:57,828
Other traders noticed what he
was doing. Then everyone noticed.

142
00:08:57,828 --> 00:09:02,613
Around New Year, the stock hit its daily
limit at seven hundred and five yen.

143
00:09:02,913 --> 00:09:08,202
In March 1982, Sumitomo announced it
would develop the land next to its mine.

144
00:09:08,202 --> 00:09:10,263
Land it had acquired from him.

145
00:09:10,547 --> 00:09:14,607
In April, the shares reached one
thousand two hundred and thirty yen.

146
00:09:14,607 --> 00:09:18,372
More than five times the price on
the morning he read the paper.

147
00:09:18,680 --> 00:09:22,830
And unlike in 1935, this
time he took the money.

148
00:09:23,147 --> 00:09:26,286
The gain was reported at
about twenty billion yen.

149
00:09:26,286 --> 00:09:28,922
Around eighty million dollars at the time.

150
00:09:29,213 --> 00:09:33,030
Made in a little over six months,
by a man in his mid-eighties,

151
00:09:33,030 --> 00:09:35,938
from a short article
everyone else had read too.

152
00:09:37,247 --> 00:09:40,902
Every year, Japan published a
list of its biggest taxpayers.

153
00:09:40,902 --> 00:09:43,522
The next list put him
first in the country.

154
00:09:43,813 --> 00:09:45,046
The press loved him.

155
00:09:45,046 --> 00:09:50,165
The eighty-five-year-old with an elementary
school education who had out-read every

156
00:09:50,165 --> 00:09:51,213
analyst in Tokyo.

157
00:09:51,513 --> 00:09:54,691
He changed nothing. The
same bag. The same habits.

158
00:09:54,691 --> 00:09:57,488
He had never wanted to
live like a rich man.

159
00:09:57,780 --> 00:10:00,805
The money was not the
point. Being right was.

160
00:10:01,113 --> 00:10:06,383
A director of a Japanese investors'
association later said Korekawa studied every

161
00:10:06,383 --> 00:10:11,263
day for about thirty years, and only
stopped losing money at around eighty.

162
00:10:11,580 --> 00:10:16,543
Think about that. The greatest trade of
his life came after five decades of study,

163
00:10:16,543 --> 00:10:20,780
three lost fortunes, and a battle he
lost by not knowing when to stop.

164
00:10:22,080 --> 00:10:24,205
But a turtle does not stop.

165
00:10:24,513 --> 00:10:29,063
In 1985, his son, a geologist,
died at fifty-seven.

166
00:10:29,380 --> 00:10:31,455
Korekawa kept trading.

167
00:10:31,747 --> 00:10:36,815
According to his biographer, he took a
large position in a pharmaceutical company,

168
00:10:36,815 --> 00:10:39,472
and the shares fell.
The losses were heavy.

169
00:10:39,780 --> 00:10:42,555
And the tax office had
not finished with him.

170
00:10:42,847 --> 00:10:44,728
In 1991, at ninety-three,

171
00:10:44,728 --> 00:10:50,147
he was reported to owe six hundred
and eighty million yen in income tax.

172
00:10:50,447 --> 00:10:54,147
The tax office seized the
royalties from his autobiography.

173
00:10:54,447 --> 00:10:58,972
In that book, he wrote that not a single
sen of his stock profits remained.

174
00:11:00,280 --> 00:11:03,482
His great trade did not come
from luck at eighty-four.

175
00:11:03,482 --> 00:11:07,930
It came from three years in a library
and fifty years of reading the cycle.

176
00:11:08,247 --> 00:11:10,300
Everyone could read that article.

177
00:11:10,300 --> 00:11:13,597
Only one man had spent his
life getting ready for it.

178
00:11:13,880 --> 00:11:18,370
But the same patience that let him wait
for the cycle kept bringing him back for

179
00:11:18,370 --> 00:11:22,130
one more. He could wait for years
to buy. He never learned to stop.

180
00:11:23,447 --> 00:11:28,980
Ginzō Korekawa died on the twelfth of
September 1992. He was ninety-five.

181
00:11:28,980 --> 00:11:30,572
He left a small flat.

182
00:11:30,880 --> 00:11:34,655
His foundation still pays for
children's education today.

183
00:11:34,947 --> 00:11:38,297
And the hill he went to see
is still producing gold.

184
00:11:38,613 --> 00:11:42,074
Hishikari became Japan's
most productive gold mine,

185
00:11:42,074 --> 00:11:44,313
long after his profits were gone.

